1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,927
Hi, I'm Ryan Rover.

2
00:00:02,107 --> 00:00:04,599
Getting from zero to one hundred thousand

3
00:00:04,599 --> 00:00:07,031
dollars is the hardest climb in personal

4
00:00:07,031 --> 00:00:07,517
finance.

5
00:00:07,697 --> 00:00:10,497
Charlie Munger famously said the first hundred

6
00:00:10,497 --> 00:00:11,958
thousand is the hardest.

7
00:00:12,138 --> 00:00:14,576
But here's what almost nobody tells you.

8
00:00:14,756 --> 00:00:16,056
There's an order to it.

9
00:00:16,236 --> 00:00:18,063
Do the steps in the right order,

10
00:00:18,063 --> 00:00:19,947
and every step makes the next one

11
00:00:19,947 --> 00:00:20,346
easier.

12
00:00:20,526 --> 00:00:22,034
Do them in the wrong order,

13
00:00:22,034 --> 00:00:23,766
and you can work hard for years

14
00:00:23,766 --> 00:00:24,659
and barely move.

15
00:00:25,010 --> 00:00:27,517
From zero to one hundred thousand dollars.

16
00:00:27,697 --> 00:00:29,508
Twelve steps, in order.

17
00:00:29,688 --> 00:00:31,634
Each one builds the floor for the

18
00:00:31,634 --> 00:00:31,929
next.

19
00:00:32,280 --> 00:00:34,207
Most people do it backwards.

20
00:00:34,387 --> 00:00:36,874
They start investing before they have any

21
00:00:36,874 --> 00:00:38,147
cash for emergencies,

22
00:00:38,147 --> 00:00:40,391
so the first surprise bill sends them

23
00:00:40,391 --> 00:00:41,422
to a credit card.

24
00:00:41,602 --> 00:00:43,434
Or they pay off a low interest

25
00:00:43,434 --> 00:00:45,938
loan early while their high interest card

26
00:00:45,938 --> 00:00:46,304
grows.

27
00:00:46,484 --> 00:00:48,431
Or they skip free money from their

28
00:00:48,431 --> 00:00:51,007
employer because they think they can't afford

29
00:00:51,007 --> 00:00:51,465
to save.

30
00:00:51,645 --> 00:00:53,526
Each mistake alone costs a little.

31
00:00:53,706 --> 00:00:55,517
Together, they cost years.

32
00:00:55,868 --> 00:00:57,585
Today we'll go step by step,

33
00:00:57,585 --> 00:00:59,794
in order, from the very first dollar

34
00:00:59,794 --> 00:01:01,266
to one hundred thousand.

35
00:01:01,446 --> 00:01:03,271
And we'll look at the whole journey

36
00:01:03,271 --> 00:01:04,209
from three angles.

37
00:01:04,389 --> 00:01:05,590
First the numbers:

38
00:01:05,590 --> 00:01:07,257
how long it really takes.

39
00:01:07,437 --> 00:01:08,695
Then the psychology:

40
00:01:08,695 --> 00:01:10,960
why most people quit around the same

41
00:01:10,960 --> 00:01:11,338
point.

42
00:01:11,518 --> 00:01:12,867
And then the playbook:

43
00:01:12,867 --> 00:01:15,628
three real paths for three different incomes.

44
00:01:15,978 --> 00:01:17,916
If you're starting from zero,

45
00:01:17,916 --> 00:01:19,186
or even below zero,

46
00:01:19,186 --> 00:01:20,656
this video is for you.

47
00:01:20,836 --> 00:01:21,498
Let's go.

48
00:01:21,849 --> 00:01:24,159
Our numbers come from named sources.

49
00:01:24,339 --> 00:01:27,187
Household savings data comes from the Federal

50
00:01:27,187 --> 00:01:29,909
Reserve's survey on the economic well being

51
00:01:29,909 --> 00:01:30,225
of U.

52
00:01:30,405 --> 00:01:30,777
S.

53
00:01:30,957 --> 00:01:31,851
households.

54
00:01:32,031 --> 00:01:34,516
Net worth data comes from the Federal

55
00:01:34,516 --> 00:01:37,069
Reserve's Survey of Consumer Finances.

56
00:01:37,249 --> 00:01:39,627
Credit card rates come from the Federal

57
00:01:39,627 --> 00:01:41,638
Reserve's consumer credit report.

58
00:01:41,818 --> 00:01:44,738
Retirement account rules come from the IRS,

59
00:01:44,738 --> 00:01:46,978
and the limits change every year,

60
00:01:46,978 --> 00:01:49,423
so always check the current numbers.

61
00:01:49,773 --> 00:01:52,654
Growth examples use a seven percent average

62
00:01:52,654 --> 00:01:54,932
yearly return, compounded monthly,

63
00:01:54,932 --> 00:01:56,204
as an illustration.

64
00:01:56,384 --> 00:01:57,604
That's not a promise.

65
00:01:57,784 --> 00:01:59,536
Markets go up and down,

66
00:01:59,536 --> 00:02:00,756
sometimes a lot.

67
00:02:00,936 --> 00:02:02,291
This is education,

68
00:02:02,291 --> 00:02:03,873
not financial advice.

69
00:02:04,053 --> 00:02:05,049
Use it as a map,

70
00:02:05,049 --> 00:02:06,979
and adjust it to your own life.

71
00:02:07,329 --> 00:02:08,083
Angle one.

72
00:02:08,263 --> 00:02:09,076
The numbers.

73
00:02:09,256 --> 00:02:10,355
Before the steps,

74
00:02:10,355 --> 00:02:12,682
let's see exactly how long the climb

75
00:02:12,682 --> 00:02:15,074
takes, because once you see the math,

76
00:02:15,074 --> 00:02:16,884
it stops feeling impossible.

77
00:02:17,234 --> 00:02:19,114
At a seven percent illustration,

78
00:02:19,114 --> 00:02:21,640
investing five hundred dollars a month gets

79
00:02:21,640 --> 00:02:23,873
you to one hundred thousand dollars in

80
00:02:23,873 --> 00:02:24,989
about eleven years.

81
00:02:25,169 --> 00:02:26,900
One thousand a month gets you there

82
00:02:26,900 --> 00:02:27,642
in under seven.

83
00:02:27,992 --> 00:02:29,211
Notice something.

84
00:02:29,391 --> 00:02:31,909
Doubling your monthly amount doesn't cut the

85
00:02:31,909 --> 00:02:32,653
time in half.

86
00:02:32,833 --> 00:02:34,678
It cuts it from about eleven years

87
00:02:34,678 --> 00:02:35,492
to under seven.

88
00:02:35,672 --> 00:02:37,352
That's because at the start,

89
00:02:37,352 --> 00:02:39,332
almost all of the balance is your

90
00:02:39,332 --> 00:02:39,933
own money.

91
00:02:40,113 --> 00:02:42,423
Growth only becomes big later.

92
00:02:42,603 --> 00:02:44,119
At five hundred a month,

93
00:02:44,119 --> 00:02:46,457
you'd put in about sixty six thousand

94
00:02:46,457 --> 00:02:47,595
of your own money,

95
00:02:47,595 --> 00:02:49,174
and growth adds the rest.

96
00:02:49,525 --> 00:02:50,511
Flip it around.

97
00:02:50,691 --> 00:02:52,917
If you want one hundred thousand dollars

98
00:02:52,917 --> 00:02:53,640
in ten years,

99
00:02:53,640 --> 00:02:55,809
you'd need to invest about five hundred

100
00:02:55,809 --> 00:02:57,089
eighty dollars a month.

101
00:02:57,269 --> 00:02:58,249
In seven years,

102
00:02:58,249 --> 00:03:00,276
about nine hundred twenty five.

103
00:03:00,456 --> 00:03:01,391
In five years,

104
00:03:01,391 --> 00:03:02,928
about fourteen hundred.

105
00:03:03,279 --> 00:03:05,125
Those numbers might look big.

106
00:03:05,305 --> 00:03:07,459
But remember, they don't have to come

107
00:03:07,459 --> 00:03:08,857
only from cutting costs.

108
00:03:09,037 --> 00:03:10,709
Employer matches count.

109
00:03:10,889 --> 00:03:12,004
Raises count.

110
00:03:12,184 --> 00:03:14,097
A paid off car loan that you

111
00:03:14,097 --> 00:03:15,191
redirect counts.

112
00:03:15,371 --> 00:03:17,857
Most people who reach one hundred thousand

113
00:03:17,857 --> 00:03:19,218
do it with a mix of all

114
00:03:19,218 --> 00:03:21,408
of those, not with one heroic budget.

115
00:03:21,758 --> 00:03:24,323
Now here's where most people actually start.

116
00:03:24,503 --> 00:03:27,098
The Federal Reserve found that sixty three

117
00:03:27,098 --> 00:03:29,569
percent of adults would cover a surprise

118
00:03:29,569 --> 00:03:32,040
four hundred dollar expense with cash or

119
00:03:32,040 --> 00:03:32,967
its equivalent.

120
00:03:33,147 --> 00:03:34,912
That means more than a third couldn't.

121
00:03:35,092 --> 00:03:36,816
And credit card interest rates,

122
00:03:36,816 --> 00:03:38,651
according to the Federal Reserve,

123
00:03:38,651 --> 00:03:40,374
have been above twenty percent.

124
00:03:40,554 --> 00:03:41,834
So for many people,

125
00:03:41,834 --> 00:03:44,664
step zero is simply stopping the bleeding.

126
00:03:45,015 --> 00:03:46,988
One more number before the steps:

127
00:03:46,988 --> 00:03:48,184
the cost of waiting.

128
00:03:48,364 --> 00:03:50,187
If you put off starting for just

129
00:03:50,187 --> 00:03:52,523
three years, you don't arrive three years

130
00:03:52,523 --> 00:03:53,890
later at the same place.

131
00:03:54,070 --> 00:03:56,221
You lose the three years of growth

132
00:03:56,221 --> 00:03:57,233
at the very end,

133
00:03:57,233 --> 00:03:59,005
when the balance is largest.

134
00:03:59,185 --> 00:04:01,100
That's why the best time to start

135
00:04:01,100 --> 00:04:02,494
this road is always now,

136
00:04:02,494 --> 00:04:03,945
even with a small amount.

137
00:04:04,125 --> 00:04:06,530
Twenty five dollars a month started today

138
00:04:06,530 --> 00:04:08,817
often beats two hundred dollars a month

139
00:04:08,817 --> 00:04:09,756
started someday.

140
00:04:10,106 --> 00:04:11,904
And the last number is the one

141
00:04:11,904 --> 00:04:13,763
that makes all the others work:

142
00:04:13,763 --> 00:04:14,483
consistency.

143
00:04:14,663 --> 00:04:16,561
A plan you follow for ten years

144
00:04:16,561 --> 00:04:18,827
at five hundred dollars a month beats

145
00:04:18,827 --> 00:04:20,726
a plan for one thousand a month

146
00:04:20,726 --> 00:04:23,115
that you abandon after eighteen months.

147
00:04:23,295 --> 00:04:25,359
When you choose your monthly amount,

148
00:04:25,359 --> 00:04:27,252
choose one you can keep through a

149
00:04:27,252 --> 00:04:29,030
bad month, not just a good one.

150
00:04:29,210 --> 00:04:30,777
You can always raise it later.

151
00:04:31,128 --> 00:04:31,546
Okay.

152
00:04:31,726 --> 00:04:33,245
Now the twelve steps,

153
00:04:33,245 --> 00:04:33,897
in order.

154
00:04:34,077 --> 00:04:36,077
Step one takes about one evening,

155
00:04:36,077 --> 00:04:38,198
and it changes everything after it.

156
00:04:38,548 --> 00:04:39,325
Step one.

157
00:04:39,505 --> 00:04:40,736
Know your numbers.

158
00:04:40,916 --> 00:04:42,649
You can't plan a trip without knowing

159
00:04:42,649 --> 00:04:43,679
where you're starting.

160
00:04:44,030 --> 00:04:45,144
Two numbers matter.

161
00:04:45,324 --> 00:04:46,250
Your net worth,

162
00:04:46,250 --> 00:04:48,964
which is everything you own minus everything

163
00:04:48,964 --> 00:04:49,457
you owe.

164
00:04:49,637 --> 00:04:51,309
And your monthly cash flow,

165
00:04:51,309 --> 00:04:53,353
which is what comes in minus what

166
00:04:53,353 --> 00:04:53,910
goes out.

167
00:04:54,090 --> 00:04:56,263
Most people have never written either of

168
00:04:56,263 --> 00:04:56,807
them down.

169
00:04:56,987 --> 00:04:59,217
They have a feeling about their money,

170
00:04:59,217 --> 00:05:01,037
and feelings are usually wrong,

171
00:05:01,037 --> 00:05:02,153
in both directions.

172
00:05:02,503 --> 00:05:03,199
Here's how.

173
00:05:03,379 --> 00:05:04,949
Open every account:

174
00:05:04,949 --> 00:05:07,427
checking, savings, retirement,

175
00:05:07,427 --> 00:05:09,080
credit cards, loans.

176
00:05:09,260 --> 00:05:10,711
Write down each balance.

177
00:05:10,891 --> 00:05:12,179
Add up what you own,

178
00:05:12,179 --> 00:05:13,596
subtract what you owe.

179
00:05:13,776 --> 00:05:15,806
Then look at the last three months

180
00:05:15,806 --> 00:05:18,492
of bank statements and calculate your average

181
00:05:18,492 --> 00:05:19,686
income and spending.

182
00:05:19,866 --> 00:05:21,050
It takes one evening.

183
00:05:21,230 --> 00:05:22,228
It's not fun.

184
00:05:22,408 --> 00:05:24,800
But it's the foundation of everything else.

185
00:05:25,150 --> 00:05:27,078
Say you discover your net worth is

186
00:05:27,078 --> 00:05:28,722
minus eight thousand dollars,

187
00:05:28,722 --> 00:05:30,821
and you have two hundred dollars left

188
00:05:30,821 --> 00:05:32,522
at the end of a typical month.

189
00:05:32,702 --> 00:05:33,967
That's not bad news.

190
00:05:34,147 --> 00:05:35,366
That's a starting line.

191
00:05:35,546 --> 00:05:37,587
You now know exactly how much fuel

192
00:05:37,587 --> 00:05:39,868
you have, two hundred dollars a month,

193
00:05:39,868 --> 00:05:41,729
and exactly how far you have to

194
00:05:41,729 --> 00:05:41,909
go.

195
00:05:42,089 --> 00:05:44,024
People who track their numbers tend to

196
00:05:44,024 --> 00:05:45,145
make better decisions,

197
00:05:45,145 --> 00:05:46,826
simply because they can see them.

198
00:05:47,176 --> 00:05:48,174
Step two.

199
00:05:48,354 --> 00:05:50,220
Build a small emergency fund,

200
00:05:50,220 --> 00:05:53,052
around one thousand to two thousand dollars,

201
00:05:53,052 --> 00:05:54,403
before anything else.

202
00:05:54,753 --> 00:05:55,658
Why first?

203
00:05:55,838 --> 00:05:57,019
Because without it,

204
00:05:57,019 --> 00:05:59,008
every surprise becomes new debt.

205
00:05:59,188 --> 00:06:00,148
The flat tire,

206
00:06:00,148 --> 00:06:02,206
the dentist, the broken phone.

207
00:06:02,386 --> 00:06:03,788
If you put that on a card

208
00:06:03,788 --> 00:06:05,079
at over twenty percent,

209
00:06:05,079 --> 00:06:06,369
you're going backwards.

210
00:06:06,549 --> 00:06:08,754
A small cushion breaks that cycle.

211
00:06:08,934 --> 00:06:10,565
It's not meant to cover a job

212
00:06:10,565 --> 00:06:11,071
loss yet.

213
00:06:11,251 --> 00:06:13,359
It's meant to cover the normal chaos

214
00:06:13,359 --> 00:06:13,828
of life.

215
00:06:14,178 --> 00:06:16,077
Open a separate savings account,

216
00:06:16,077 --> 00:06:17,739
ideally at a different bank,

217
00:06:17,739 --> 00:06:19,638
so it's not sitting next to your

218
00:06:19,638 --> 00:06:20,528
spending money.

219
00:06:20,708 --> 00:06:23,030
Set an automatic transfer on payday.

220
00:06:23,210 --> 00:06:24,836
Sell a few things you don't use.

221
00:06:25,016 --> 00:06:26,606
Pick up a few extra shifts.

222
00:06:26,786 --> 00:06:28,017
The goal is speed.

223
00:06:28,197 --> 00:06:30,279
Most people can build this in one

224
00:06:30,279 --> 00:06:32,109
to four months if they focus.

225
00:06:32,460 --> 00:06:33,748
Here's what changes.

226
00:06:33,928 --> 00:06:35,592
The next time the car needs a

227
00:06:35,592 --> 00:06:37,199
three hundred dollar repair,

228
00:06:37,199 --> 00:06:38,806
you pay it from the cushion,

229
00:06:38,806 --> 00:06:39,896
and then refill it.

230
00:06:40,076 --> 00:06:40,738
No new debt.

231
00:06:40,918 --> 00:06:41,684
No panic.

232
00:06:41,864 --> 00:06:42,811
It sounds small,

233
00:06:42,811 --> 00:06:44,647
but for many people this is the

234
00:06:44,647 --> 00:06:47,015
first time money feels like it's working

235
00:06:47,015 --> 00:06:48,969
for them instead of against them.

236
00:06:49,319 --> 00:06:50,259
Step three.

237
00:06:50,439 --> 00:06:53,393
If your employer matches retirement contributions,

238
00:06:53,393 --> 00:06:55,756
contribute enough to get the full match.

239
00:06:56,107 --> 00:06:58,679
Why this comes before paying off debt:

240
00:06:58,679 --> 00:07:00,508
a match is often a fifty or

241
00:07:00,508 --> 00:07:03,351
one hundred percent instant return on your

242
00:07:03,351 --> 00:07:03,758
money.

243
00:07:03,938 --> 00:07:06,508
A common formula is fifty cents for

244
00:07:06,508 --> 00:07:08,271
every dollar you put in,

245
00:07:08,271 --> 00:07:10,695
up to six percent of your salary.

246
00:07:10,875 --> 00:07:13,635
No investment and no debt payoff beats

247
00:07:13,635 --> 00:07:15,960
an instant fifty percent return.

248
00:07:16,140 --> 00:07:18,107
Skipping it is turning down part of

249
00:07:18,107 --> 00:07:18,613
your pay.

250
00:07:18,963 --> 00:07:21,383
Look up your plan's matching rule in

251
00:07:21,383 --> 00:07:22,795
your benefits portal,

252
00:07:22,795 --> 00:07:23,467
or ask HR.

253
00:07:23,647 --> 00:07:25,704
Then set your contribution to at least

254
00:07:25,704 --> 00:07:27,978
the level that gets every matching dollar.

255
00:07:28,158 --> 00:07:29,274
If money is tight,

256
00:07:29,274 --> 00:07:30,885
start at whatever you can,

257
00:07:30,885 --> 00:07:32,931
and raise it by one percent every

258
00:07:32,931 --> 00:07:35,100
few months until you reach the full

259
00:07:35,100 --> 00:07:35,472
match.

260
00:07:35,822 --> 00:07:38,337
Say you earn fifty thousand dollars and

261
00:07:38,337 --> 00:07:40,980
your employer matches fifty percent up to

262
00:07:40,980 --> 00:07:41,754
six percent.

263
00:07:41,934 --> 00:07:43,815
You put in three thousand a year.

264
00:07:43,995 --> 00:07:46,247
Your employer adds fifteen hundred.

265
00:07:46,427 --> 00:07:49,044
That's fifteen hundred dollars a year you'd

266
00:07:49,044 --> 00:07:50,627
lose by not participating,

267
00:07:50,627 --> 00:07:51,966
and over thirty years,

268
00:07:51,966 --> 00:07:54,157
with growth, that alone could add up

269
00:07:54,157 --> 00:07:55,982
to far more than you'd expect.

270
00:07:56,333 --> 00:07:57,273
Step four.

271
00:07:57,453 --> 00:07:59,371
Pay off high interest debt,

272
00:07:59,371 --> 00:08:01,075
especially credit cards.

273
00:08:01,426 --> 00:08:04,386
Credit card balances often charge over twenty

274
00:08:04,386 --> 00:08:05,373
percent a year.

275
00:08:05,553 --> 00:08:07,577
Paying off a card at twenty two

276
00:08:07,577 --> 00:08:10,385
percent is like earning a guaranteed twenty

277
00:08:10,385 --> 00:08:11,625
two percent return.

278
00:08:11,805 --> 00:08:14,679
Nothing in the investing world reliably beats

279
00:08:14,679 --> 00:08:14,998
that.

280
00:08:15,178 --> 00:08:17,763
So until high interest debt is gone,

281
00:08:17,763 --> 00:08:20,205
it's the best investment you have.

282
00:08:20,555 --> 00:08:22,180
There are two popular methods.

283
00:08:22,360 --> 00:08:25,114
The avalanche: pay minimums on everything,

284
00:08:25,114 --> 00:08:27,409
and throw every extra dollar at the

285
00:08:27,409 --> 00:08:29,245
highest interest rate first.

286
00:08:29,425 --> 00:08:30,760
It saves the most money.

287
00:08:30,940 --> 00:08:33,587
The snowball: pay off the smallest balance

288
00:08:33,587 --> 00:08:34,910
first for quick wins.

289
00:08:35,090 --> 00:08:36,402
It can keep you motivated.

290
00:08:36,582 --> 00:08:39,145
Either works far better than paying minimums

291
00:08:39,145 --> 00:08:39,961
on everything.

292
00:08:40,141 --> 00:08:42,068
Pick the one you'll actually stick to.

293
00:08:42,418 --> 00:08:44,134
Say you have three cards,

294
00:08:44,134 --> 00:08:46,195
with balances of two thousand,

295
00:08:46,195 --> 00:08:48,873
four thousand and six thousand dollars.

296
00:08:49,053 --> 00:08:50,619
With the avalanche method,

297
00:08:50,619 --> 00:08:53,210
you'd target whichever card has the highest

298
00:08:53,210 --> 00:08:55,740
rate, while paying minimums on the others.

299
00:08:55,920 --> 00:08:56,773
When it's gone,

300
00:08:56,773 --> 00:08:58,932
that whole payment rolls into the next

301
00:08:58,932 --> 00:08:59,159
one.

302
00:08:59,339 --> 00:09:01,464
Each payoff speeds up the next.

303
00:09:01,644 --> 00:09:03,970
People often describe the last card as

304
00:09:03,970 --> 00:09:04,949
the fastest one,

305
00:09:04,949 --> 00:09:07,764
because by then they're throwing everything at

306
00:09:07,764 --> 00:09:07,948
it.

307
00:09:08,298 --> 00:09:09,308
Step five.

308
00:09:09,488 --> 00:09:12,020
Grow your emergency fund to three to

309
00:09:12,020 --> 00:09:14,341
six months of essential expenses.

310
00:09:14,691 --> 00:09:17,285
Your starter fund protects you from small

311
00:09:17,285 --> 00:09:17,918
surprises.

312
00:09:18,098 --> 00:09:20,959
This one protects you from big ones:

313
00:09:20,959 --> 00:09:21,833
a job loss,

314
00:09:21,833 --> 00:09:23,264
a medical problem,

315
00:09:23,264 --> 00:09:24,774
a family emergency.

316
00:09:24,954 --> 00:09:27,496
With three to six months of essentials

317
00:09:27,496 --> 00:09:29,837
saved, a layoff becomes a hard time

318
00:09:29,837 --> 00:09:31,978
instead of a financial disaster.

319
00:09:32,158 --> 00:09:34,109
And it means you'll never have to

320
00:09:34,109 --> 00:09:36,061
sell investments at a bad time to

321
00:09:36,061 --> 00:09:36,593
pay rent.

322
00:09:36,943 --> 00:09:39,739
Add up your essential monthly costs:

323
00:09:39,739 --> 00:09:41,681
housing, food, utilities,

324
00:09:41,681 --> 00:09:44,710
insurance, transport, minimum payments.

325
00:09:44,890 --> 00:09:46,968
Multiply by three to six.

326
00:09:47,148 --> 00:09:49,352
If your income is very stable,

327
00:09:49,352 --> 00:09:50,968
three might be enough.

328
00:09:51,148 --> 00:09:54,282
If you're self employed or your industry

329
00:09:54,282 --> 00:09:57,103
is unpredictable, aim closer to six.

330
00:09:57,283 --> 00:09:59,272
Keep it in a high yield savings

331
00:09:59,272 --> 00:10:01,068
account that's FDIC insured.

332
00:10:01,419 --> 00:10:04,099
Say your essentials are three thousand dollars

333
00:10:04,099 --> 00:10:04,565
a month.

334
00:10:04,745 --> 00:10:07,246
Your target is somewhere between nine and

335
00:10:07,246 --> 00:10:08,832
eighteen thousand dollars.

336
00:10:09,012 --> 00:10:10,068
That sounds like a lot.

337
00:10:10,248 --> 00:10:12,230
But if you just finished paying off

338
00:10:12,230 --> 00:10:14,494
debt, you already have a monthly payment

339
00:10:14,494 --> 00:10:14,834
habit.

340
00:10:15,014 --> 00:10:16,698
Keep making the same payment,

341
00:10:16,698 --> 00:10:17,684
just to yourself.

342
00:10:17,864 --> 00:10:19,881
Many people fill this fund faster than

343
00:10:19,881 --> 00:10:21,208
they paid off their debt.

344
00:10:21,558 --> 00:10:22,765
Step six.

345
00:10:22,945 --> 00:10:25,261
Automate your saving and push your savings

346
00:10:25,261 --> 00:10:27,357
rate toward fifteen to twenty percent.

347
00:10:27,707 --> 00:10:29,251
Willpower runs out.

348
00:10:29,431 --> 00:10:30,627
Automation doesn't.

349
00:10:30,807 --> 00:10:33,367
When money moves to savings and investments

350
00:10:33,367 --> 00:10:34,795
automatically on payday,

351
00:10:34,795 --> 00:10:35,867
before you see it,

352
00:10:35,867 --> 00:10:38,249
you adjust your spending to what's left.

353
00:10:38,429 --> 00:10:41,107
That's the single most reliable way people

354
00:10:41,107 --> 00:10:43,212
build wealth on ordinary incomes.

355
00:10:43,392 --> 00:10:44,672
And your savings rate,

356
00:10:44,672 --> 00:10:46,651
the share of your income you keep,

357
00:10:46,651 --> 00:10:48,222
decides how fast you climb.

358
00:10:48,572 --> 00:10:51,645
Set up automatic contributions to your retirement

359
00:10:51,645 --> 00:10:53,151
account through payroll,

360
00:10:53,151 --> 00:10:56,036
and automatic transfers to your other accounts

361
00:10:56,036 --> 00:10:56,664
on payday.

362
00:10:56,844 --> 00:10:58,424
Then use a simple rule:

363
00:10:58,424 --> 00:11:00,279
every time you get a raise,

364
00:11:00,279 --> 00:11:02,134
send at least half of it to

365
00:11:02,134 --> 00:11:02,684
savings.

366
00:11:02,864 --> 00:11:04,701
Your lifestyle still improves,

367
00:11:04,701 --> 00:11:07,089
but your rate climbs every year without

368
00:11:07,089 --> 00:11:09,110
it ever feeling like a sacrifice.

369
00:11:09,460 --> 00:11:12,025
Say you earn sixty thousand dollars and

370
00:11:12,025 --> 00:11:13,210
save five percent.

371
00:11:13,390 --> 00:11:14,852
That's three thousand a year.

372
00:11:15,032 --> 00:11:17,043
Each year you get a three percent

373
00:11:17,043 --> 00:11:18,748
raise and send half of it to

374
00:11:18,748 --> 00:11:19,235
savings.

375
00:11:19,415 --> 00:11:20,850
After about seven years,

376
00:11:20,850 --> 00:11:22,941
without ever cutting your spending,

377
00:11:22,941 --> 00:11:25,093
you're saving well over ten percent.

378
00:11:25,273 --> 00:11:27,643
That's the quiet power of automating your

379
00:11:27,643 --> 00:11:28,047
raises.

380
00:11:28,398 --> 00:11:29,408
Quick pause.

381
00:11:29,588 --> 00:11:31,524
If you're finding this step by step

382
00:11:31,524 --> 00:11:33,349
useful, subscribe to the channel.

383
00:11:33,529 --> 00:11:35,574
I'm making a full series on every

384
00:11:35,574 --> 00:11:37,556
stage, from zero to one million,

385
00:11:37,556 --> 00:11:39,724
and subscribing is the best way not

386
00:11:39,724 --> 00:11:41,087
to miss the next step.

387
00:11:41,267 --> 00:11:42,812
Now, step seven.

388
00:11:43,162 --> 00:11:44,137
Step seven.

389
00:11:44,317 --> 00:11:46,865
Open an individual retirement account,

390
00:11:46,865 --> 00:11:49,414
an IRA, alongside your workplace plan.

391
00:11:49,764 --> 00:11:52,237
An IRA is a retirement account you

392
00:11:52,237 --> 00:11:54,419
open yourself, at a brokerage.

393
00:11:54,599 --> 00:11:55,911
There are two main types.

394
00:11:56,091 --> 00:11:58,418
A traditional IRA may give you a

395
00:11:58,418 --> 00:11:59,727
tax deduction now,

396
00:11:59,727 --> 00:12:01,327
and you pay tax later.

397
00:12:01,507 --> 00:12:04,420
A Roth IRA uses money you've already

398
00:12:04,420 --> 00:12:05,391
paid tax on,

399
00:12:05,391 --> 00:12:09,195
and qualified withdrawals in retirement are tax

400
00:12:09,195 --> 00:12:09,599
free.

401
00:12:09,779 --> 00:12:12,618
Both let your investments grow without yearly

402
00:12:12,618 --> 00:12:13,564
taxes on gains.

403
00:12:13,915 --> 00:12:14,542
Which one?

404
00:12:14,722 --> 00:12:17,232
Many people early in their careers,

405
00:12:17,232 --> 00:12:18,809
in lower tax brackets,

406
00:12:18,809 --> 00:12:19,957
prefer the Roth,

407
00:12:19,957 --> 00:12:22,036
because they pay tax now at a

408
00:12:22,036 --> 00:12:22,825
lower rate.

409
00:12:23,005 --> 00:12:25,917
Income limits apply to Roth contributions,

410
00:12:25,917 --> 00:12:27,927
and the yearly limits change,

411
00:12:27,927 --> 00:12:30,076
so check the current IRS rules.

412
00:12:30,256 --> 00:12:31,409
Open the account,

413
00:12:31,409 --> 00:12:34,190
set up an automatic monthly contribution,

414
00:12:34,190 --> 00:12:35,751
and, this is important,

415
00:12:35,751 --> 00:12:38,464
actually choose an investment inside it.

416
00:12:38,644 --> 00:12:40,627
Money sitting as cash in an IRA

417
00:12:40,627 --> 00:12:41,779
doesn't grow much.

418
00:12:42,129 --> 00:12:43,910
That last point catches a lot of

419
00:12:43,910 --> 00:12:44,300
people.

420
00:12:44,480 --> 00:12:45,682
They open an IRA,

421
00:12:45,682 --> 00:12:46,956
transfer money in,

422
00:12:46,956 --> 00:12:49,008
and never pick an investment.

423
00:12:49,188 --> 00:12:51,957
Years later, they discover it's been sitting

424
00:12:51,957 --> 00:12:53,971
in cash, earning almost nothing.

425
00:12:54,151 --> 00:12:56,628
Try this: if you already have an

426
00:12:56,628 --> 00:12:59,104
IRA, log in today and check what

427
00:12:59,104 --> 00:13:01,117
it's actually invested in.

428
00:13:01,467 --> 00:13:02,233
Step eight.

429
00:13:02,413 --> 00:13:03,736
Invest simply.

430
00:13:03,916 --> 00:13:06,645
Broad, low cost index funds.

431
00:13:06,995 --> 00:13:09,333
An index fund buys a whole market,

432
00:13:09,333 --> 00:13:11,534
like the five hundred largest U.

433
00:13:11,714 --> 00:13:12,062
S.

434
00:13:12,242 --> 00:13:14,274
companies, or the entire U.

435
00:13:14,454 --> 00:13:14,802
S.

436
00:13:14,982 --> 00:13:17,526
stock market, instead of trying to pick

437
00:13:17,526 --> 00:13:18,047
winners.

438
00:13:18,227 --> 00:13:20,892
Research, like S and P's long running

439
00:13:20,892 --> 00:13:24,133
SPIVA reports, has repeatedly found that most

440
00:13:24,133 --> 00:13:27,734
actively managed funds trail their benchmark index

441
00:13:27,734 --> 00:13:29,031
over long periods,

442
00:13:29,031 --> 00:13:30,615
especially after fees.

443
00:13:30,966 --> 00:13:33,634
A simple approach many investors use is

444
00:13:33,634 --> 00:13:36,028
one or two broad stock index funds,

445
00:13:36,028 --> 00:13:38,081
plus a bond fund that grows as

446
00:13:38,081 --> 00:13:40,544
you get closer to needing the money.

447
00:13:40,724 --> 00:13:42,347
Or a single target date fund that

448
00:13:42,347 --> 00:13:43,429
does that mix for you.

449
00:13:43,609 --> 00:13:46,024
Look for low expense ratios.

450
00:13:46,204 --> 00:13:47,089
The less you pay,

451
00:13:47,089 --> 00:13:48,027
the more you keep.

452
00:13:48,207 --> 00:13:49,495
And keep it boring.

453
00:13:49,675 --> 00:13:50,999
Boring is the point.

454
00:13:51,349 --> 00:13:53,304
Here's why cost matters so much on

455
00:13:53,304 --> 00:13:55,145
the way to one hundred thousand.

456
00:13:55,325 --> 00:13:57,299
A fund charging one percent a year

457
00:13:57,299 --> 00:13:59,563
versus one charging a small fraction of

458
00:13:59,563 --> 00:14:01,711
a percent might not look different in

459
00:14:01,711 --> 00:14:02,233
year one.

460
00:14:02,413 --> 00:14:03,427
But over decades,

461
00:14:03,427 --> 00:14:05,872
that fee difference compounds into a very

462
00:14:05,872 --> 00:14:07,185
large amount of money.

463
00:14:07,365 --> 00:14:09,289
Same market, same years,

464
00:14:09,289 --> 00:14:10,813
less money for you.

465
00:14:11,163 --> 00:14:12,068
Step nine.

466
00:14:12,248 --> 00:14:13,999
Control the big three:

467
00:14:13,999 --> 00:14:16,625
housing, transportation and food.

468
00:14:16,975 --> 00:14:20,050
According to the Bureau of Labor Statistics,

469
00:14:20,050 --> 00:14:23,476
housing, transportation and food together make up

470
00:14:23,476 --> 00:14:26,621
the largest share of what American households

471
00:14:26,621 --> 00:14:27,040
spend.

472
00:14:27,220 --> 00:14:29,275
That means a ten percent cut in

473
00:14:29,275 --> 00:14:31,330
one of these is worth more than

474
00:14:31,330 --> 00:14:34,047
cancelling dozens of small subscriptions.

475
00:14:34,227 --> 00:14:35,562
Small savings help.

476
00:14:35,742 --> 00:14:37,774
The big three move the needle.

477
00:14:38,124 --> 00:14:40,496
On housing, think about a roommate,

478
00:14:40,496 --> 00:14:41,580
a smaller place,

479
00:14:41,580 --> 00:14:43,545
or a slightly longer commute.

480
00:14:43,725 --> 00:14:46,625
On transportation, keep cars longer,

481
00:14:46,625 --> 00:14:48,478
buy reliable used ones,

482
00:14:48,478 --> 00:14:50,169
and avoid long loans.

483
00:14:50,349 --> 00:14:53,379
On food, cook more and plan meals.

484
00:14:53,559 --> 00:14:55,033
You don't need to do all of

485
00:14:55,033 --> 00:14:55,196
it.

486
00:14:55,376 --> 00:14:57,323
One meaningful change in one of the

487
00:14:57,323 --> 00:14:59,436
big three can free hundreds of dollars

488
00:14:59,436 --> 00:14:59,881
a month.

489
00:15:00,231 --> 00:15:02,197
Say you keep your current car for

490
00:15:02,197 --> 00:15:04,402
four more years instead of trading it

491
00:15:04,402 --> 00:15:05,773
in for a new one with a

492
00:15:05,773 --> 00:15:07,382
six hundred dollar payment.

493
00:15:07,562 --> 00:15:09,808
That's over twenty eight thousand dollars you

494
00:15:09,808 --> 00:15:10,407
don't spend.

495
00:15:10,587 --> 00:15:13,665
Invested instead, that money alone could cover

496
00:15:13,665 --> 00:15:15,405
a big chunk of the road to

497
00:15:15,405 --> 00:15:16,810
one hundred thousand.

498
00:15:17,160 --> 00:15:18,100
Step ten.

499
00:15:18,280 --> 00:15:19,325
Grow your income.

500
00:15:19,505 --> 00:15:20,654
Cutting has a floor.

501
00:15:20,834 --> 00:15:22,146
Earning has no ceiling.

502
00:15:22,497 --> 00:15:24,180
There's only so much you can cut.

503
00:15:24,360 --> 00:15:25,853
But there's no limit to how much

504
00:15:25,853 --> 00:15:26,833
your income can grow.

505
00:15:27,013 --> 00:15:28,533
A raise, a promotion,

506
00:15:28,533 --> 00:15:29,257
a new job,

507
00:15:29,257 --> 00:15:31,936
a certification, or a side income can

508
00:15:31,936 --> 00:15:34,977
change your timeline more than any budget.

509
00:15:35,157 --> 00:15:37,582
Studies of wage growth have found that

510
00:15:37,582 --> 00:15:40,070
people who switch jobs often see bigger

511
00:15:40,070 --> 00:15:41,856
raises than people who stay.

512
00:15:42,207 --> 00:15:43,855
Start with your current job.

513
00:15:44,035 --> 00:15:45,514
Ask what it takes to get to

514
00:15:45,514 --> 00:15:46,336
the next level,

515
00:15:46,336 --> 00:15:47,541
and get it in writing.

516
00:15:47,721 --> 00:15:49,287
Look at job listings for your role

517
00:15:49,287 --> 00:15:50,438
to see what you're worth.

518
00:15:50,618 --> 00:15:52,897
Learn one skill that's in demand in

519
00:15:52,897 --> 00:15:53,614
your field.

520
00:15:53,794 --> 00:15:55,256
And if you have time,

521
00:15:55,256 --> 00:15:56,232
a side income,

522
00:15:56,232 --> 00:15:58,670
even a few hundred dollars a month,

523
00:15:58,670 --> 00:16:00,551
can go straight to savings.

524
00:16:00,901 --> 00:16:03,730
Say a new certification bumps your salary

525
00:16:03,730 --> 00:16:05,870
by six thousand dollars a year.

526
00:16:06,050 --> 00:16:07,257
If you invest all of it,

527
00:16:07,257 --> 00:16:09,068
that's five hundred dollars a month.

528
00:16:09,248 --> 00:16:11,338
At a seven percent illustration,

529
00:16:11,338 --> 00:16:13,884
that alone gets to one hundred thousand

530
00:16:13,884 --> 00:16:15,320
in about eleven years.

531
00:16:15,500 --> 00:16:17,422
One skill, one raise,

532
00:16:17,422 --> 00:16:18,612
one decision.

533
00:16:18,962 --> 00:16:19,925
Step eleven.

534
00:16:20,105 --> 00:16:21,533
Beat lifestyle creep.

535
00:16:21,884 --> 00:16:24,667
Lifestyle creep is when your spending rises

536
00:16:24,667 --> 00:16:26,156
as fast as your income.

537
00:16:26,336 --> 00:16:27,824
A bigger apartment,

538
00:16:27,824 --> 00:16:28,764
a nicer car,

539
00:16:28,764 --> 00:16:30,016
more eating out.

540
00:16:30,196 --> 00:16:32,100
None of it feels crazy.

541
00:16:32,280 --> 00:16:34,068
Each upgrade feels earned.

542
00:16:34,248 --> 00:16:36,731
But if everything you gain gets spent,

543
00:16:36,731 --> 00:16:38,561
your net worth stands still,

544
00:16:38,561 --> 00:16:40,390
no matter how much you earn.

545
00:16:40,570 --> 00:16:42,567
It's why some high earners never get

546
00:16:42,567 --> 00:16:43,066
anywhere.

547
00:16:43,417 --> 00:16:45,588
The fix isn't to never upgrade.

548
00:16:45,768 --> 00:16:47,614
It's to upgrade on purpose.

549
00:16:47,794 --> 00:16:49,542
Pick the one or two things that

550
00:16:49,542 --> 00:16:51,064
genuinely make you happier,

551
00:16:51,064 --> 00:16:52,925
and spend on those without guilt.

552
00:16:53,105 --> 00:16:55,033
Then keep everything else the same.

553
00:16:55,213 --> 00:16:57,362
And keep the half of every raise

554
00:16:57,362 --> 00:16:58,638
rule from step six.

555
00:16:58,818 --> 00:17:00,008
Your life gets better,

556
00:17:00,008 --> 00:17:02,010
and your savings rate keeps climbing.

557
00:17:02,361 --> 00:17:04,707
Picture two coworkers who each get a

558
00:17:04,707 --> 00:17:06,401
ten thousand dollar raise.

559
00:17:06,581 --> 00:17:09,012
One upgrades their car and apartment and

560
00:17:09,012 --> 00:17:11,140
ends the year with the same savings

561
00:17:11,140 --> 00:17:11,747
as before.

562
00:17:11,927 --> 00:17:14,575
The other upgrades their apartment a little

563
00:17:14,575 --> 00:17:16,177
and invests five thousand.

564
00:17:16,357 --> 00:17:17,310
Ten years later,

565
00:17:17,310 --> 00:17:20,050
the second coworker could have roughly seventy

566
00:17:20,050 --> 00:17:21,360
thousand dollars more,

567
00:17:21,360 --> 00:17:23,266
at a seven percent illustration,

568
00:17:23,266 --> 00:17:25,946
from that single decision repeated each year.

569
00:17:26,297 --> 00:17:27,272
Step twelve.

570
00:17:27,452 --> 00:17:28,485
Stay the course.

571
00:17:28,665 --> 00:17:30,568
This is the step that decides whether

572
00:17:30,568 --> 00:17:32,624
you actually reach one hundred thousand.

573
00:17:32,974 --> 00:17:34,140
Somewhere on the way,

574
00:17:34,140 --> 00:17:35,307
the market will drop.

575
00:17:35,487 --> 00:17:36,694
Maybe ten percent,

576
00:17:36,694 --> 00:17:37,565
maybe thirty.

577
00:17:37,745 --> 00:17:40,292
Broad markets have fallen like that many

578
00:17:40,292 --> 00:17:41,629
times in history and,

579
00:17:41,629 --> 00:17:42,775
so far, recovered,

580
00:17:42,775 --> 00:17:45,640
though the past doesn't guarantee the future.

581
00:17:45,820 --> 00:17:48,123
The people who reach their goals usually

582
00:17:48,123 --> 00:17:50,023
keep investing through the drops.

583
00:17:50,203 --> 00:17:52,008
The people who don't often sell at

584
00:17:52,008 --> 00:17:53,918
the bottom and lock in their losses.

585
00:17:54,268 --> 00:17:55,775
Make a simple rule now,

586
00:17:55,775 --> 00:17:57,217
while things are calm.

587
00:17:57,397 --> 00:17:59,341
I keep investing every month,

588
00:17:59,341 --> 00:18:01,286
no matter what the news says.

589
00:18:01,466 --> 00:18:03,350
Check your accounts once a month,

590
00:18:03,350 --> 00:18:04,206
not once a day.

591
00:18:04,386 --> 00:18:05,429
And once a year,

592
00:18:05,429 --> 00:18:08,101
rebalance: if your stocks have grown much

593
00:18:08,101 --> 00:18:09,534
bigger than your plan,

594
00:18:09,534 --> 00:18:11,164
move a bit back to bonds,

595
00:18:11,164 --> 00:18:12,141
and vice versa.

596
00:18:12,321 --> 00:18:12,774
That's it.

597
00:18:12,954 --> 00:18:14,916
Discipline beats intelligence.

598
00:18:15,266 --> 00:18:16,438
Think of it this way.

599
00:18:16,618 --> 00:18:17,864
When prices fall,

600
00:18:17,864 --> 00:18:20,868
your monthly investment buys more shares.

601
00:18:21,048 --> 00:18:23,583
If you're still years away from needing

602
00:18:23,583 --> 00:18:25,599
the money, a drop is actually a

603
00:18:25,599 --> 00:18:25,924
sale.

604
00:18:26,104 --> 00:18:27,450
It doesn't feel like one.

605
00:18:27,630 --> 00:18:29,082
It feels terrible.

606
00:18:29,262 --> 00:18:31,453
But the investors who treat drops as

607
00:18:31,453 --> 00:18:33,278
sales, instead of emergencies,

608
00:18:33,278 --> 00:18:35,165
are usually the ones who get to

609
00:18:35,165 --> 00:18:36,808
one hundred thousand first.

610
00:18:37,158 --> 00:18:39,556
Every path starts with finding some money

611
00:18:39,556 --> 00:18:40,200
each month.

612
00:18:40,380 --> 00:18:42,560
So here's where people usually find their

613
00:18:42,560 --> 00:18:44,475
first three hundred dollars a month,

614
00:18:44,475 --> 00:18:45,592
without a second job.

615
00:18:45,943 --> 00:18:48,683
Start with subscriptions and memberships.

616
00:18:48,863 --> 00:18:50,644
Streaming services, apps,

617
00:18:50,644 --> 00:18:52,211
a gym you don't go to,

618
00:18:52,211 --> 00:18:54,064
software you forgot about.

619
00:18:54,244 --> 00:18:56,470
Go through your bank statement line by

620
00:18:56,470 --> 00:18:56,763
line.

621
00:18:56,943 --> 00:18:59,111
Many people find thirty to eighty dollars

622
00:18:59,111 --> 00:19:00,698
a month here in under an hour,

623
00:19:00,698 --> 00:19:02,284
and they don't miss any of it.

624
00:19:02,634 --> 00:19:04,758
Next, the bills you never question.

625
00:19:04,938 --> 00:19:06,536
Call your phone company,

626
00:19:06,536 --> 00:19:09,864
your internet provider and your insurance company,

627
00:19:09,864 --> 00:19:11,595
and ask for a better rate,

628
00:19:11,595 --> 00:19:13,924
or compare quotes from competitors.

629
00:19:14,104 --> 00:19:16,327
Prices for the same service can vary

630
00:19:16,327 --> 00:19:18,550
a lot, and loyal customers are often

631
00:19:18,550 --> 00:19:19,538
paying the most.

632
00:19:19,718 --> 00:19:21,593
An hour of phone calls can save

633
00:19:21,593 --> 00:19:24,133
another fifty to one hundred fifty dollars

634
00:19:24,133 --> 00:19:24,617
a month.

635
00:19:24,968 --> 00:19:25,734
Then food.

636
00:19:25,914 --> 00:19:27,586
Delivery apps add fees,

637
00:19:27,586 --> 00:19:30,057
tips and higher menu prices on top

638
00:19:30,057 --> 00:19:30,929
of the meal.

639
00:19:31,109 --> 00:19:33,468
Cutting delivery from twice a week to

640
00:19:33,468 --> 00:19:34,360
twice a month,

641
00:19:34,360 --> 00:19:36,399
and bringing lunch to work a few

642
00:19:36,399 --> 00:19:37,164
days a week,

643
00:19:37,164 --> 00:19:39,586
can easily free up one hundred dollars

644
00:19:39,586 --> 00:19:40,096
or more.

645
00:19:40,276 --> 00:19:41,971
You're not giving up good food.

646
00:19:42,151 --> 00:19:43,614
You're giving up the markup.

647
00:19:43,964 --> 00:19:46,399
And finally, the money you already have

648
00:19:46,399 --> 00:19:47,273
but don't see.

649
00:19:47,453 --> 00:19:48,556
A tax refund.

650
00:19:48,736 --> 00:19:49,525
A bonus.

651
00:19:49,705 --> 00:19:51,040
Cash back rewards.

652
00:19:51,220 --> 00:19:53,368
Money from selling things you don't use.

653
00:19:53,548 --> 00:19:56,139
Instead of letting these disappear into everyday

654
00:19:56,139 --> 00:19:58,514
spending, send them straight to your current

655
00:19:58,514 --> 00:19:58,784
step.

656
00:19:58,964 --> 00:20:01,076
A single tax refund can fill a

657
00:20:01,076 --> 00:20:03,469
starter emergency fund in one day.

658
00:20:03,819 --> 00:20:04,568
Add those up,

659
00:20:04,568 --> 00:20:06,642
and three hundred dollars a month is

660
00:20:06,642 --> 00:20:08,601
realistic for a lot of households,

661
00:20:08,601 --> 00:20:10,444
with no change in where you live

662
00:20:10,444 --> 00:20:11,481
or what you drive.

663
00:20:11,661 --> 00:20:13,580
At a seven percent illustration,

664
00:20:13,580 --> 00:20:15,978
three hundred dollars a month for thirty

665
00:20:15,978 --> 00:20:18,316
years grows to over three hundred sixty

666
00:20:18,316 --> 00:20:19,336
thousand dollars.

667
00:20:19,516 --> 00:20:21,531
That's what's hiding in your bank statement

668
00:20:21,531 --> 00:20:22,000
right now.

669
00:20:22,350 --> 00:20:23,418
Angle two.

670
00:20:23,598 --> 00:20:24,631
The psychology.

671
00:20:24,811 --> 00:20:27,272
Because most people who start this journey

672
00:20:27,272 --> 00:20:28,619
don't fail at step one.

673
00:20:28,799 --> 00:20:30,274
They quit somewhere in the middle.

674
00:20:30,454 --> 00:20:33,472
And it happens at surprisingly predictable points.

675
00:20:33,822 --> 00:20:35,926
The first danger zone is around ten

676
00:20:35,926 --> 00:20:37,549
to twenty thousand dollars.

677
00:20:37,729 --> 00:20:38,811
The debt is gone,

678
00:20:38,811 --> 00:20:39,957
there's a cushion,

679
00:20:39,957 --> 00:20:41,676
and the urgency disappears.

680
00:20:41,856 --> 00:20:43,073
People feel safe,

681
00:20:43,073 --> 00:20:44,863
and safe feels like done.

682
00:20:45,043 --> 00:20:47,156
This is where many people stop pushing.

683
00:20:47,336 --> 00:20:48,675
The fix is to set the next

684
00:20:48,675 --> 00:20:50,633
goal before you reach the current one.

685
00:20:50,813 --> 00:20:52,472
When you hit twenty thousand,

686
00:20:52,472 --> 00:20:54,644
you should already be aiming at fifty.

687
00:20:54,995 --> 00:20:57,278
The second danger zone is the slow

688
00:20:57,278 --> 00:20:59,964
middle, around thirty to sixty thousand.

689
00:21:00,144 --> 00:21:02,790
Growth is still small compared with your

690
00:21:02,790 --> 00:21:05,833
contributions, and every month feels the same.

691
00:21:06,013 --> 00:21:06,756
It's boring.

692
00:21:06,936 --> 00:21:09,999
Behavioral researchers have shown that people value

693
00:21:09,999 --> 00:21:12,462
rewards now much more than rewards later.

694
00:21:12,642 --> 00:21:14,702
When the reward is years away,

695
00:21:14,702 --> 00:21:15,870
motivation fades.

696
00:21:16,050 --> 00:21:18,633
The fix is to celebrate milestones:

697
00:21:18,633 --> 00:21:20,626
every ten thousand dollars,

698
00:21:20,626 --> 00:21:21,216
mark it.

699
00:21:21,396 --> 00:21:22,801
Make progress visible.

700
00:21:23,151 --> 00:21:25,508
The third danger zone is comparison.

701
00:21:25,688 --> 00:21:27,943
Around the time you're making real progress,

702
00:21:27,943 --> 00:21:30,250
you'll notice what other people are spending.

703
00:21:30,430 --> 00:21:32,404
New cars, big trips,

704
00:21:32,404 --> 00:21:33,786
bigger houses.

705
00:21:33,966 --> 00:21:35,789
It's easy to feel like you're missing

706
00:21:35,789 --> 00:21:35,986
out.

707
00:21:36,166 --> 00:21:38,342
But remember, you're seeing their spending,

708
00:21:38,342 --> 00:21:39,556
not their balance sheet.

709
00:21:39,736 --> 00:21:41,920
Many of them are on level two,

710
00:21:41,920 --> 00:21:43,230
with nicer things.

711
00:21:43,581 --> 00:21:45,858
The fourth is the first big market

712
00:21:45,858 --> 00:21:46,193
drop.

713
00:21:46,373 --> 00:21:49,477
Watching ten thousand dollars disappear from your

714
00:21:49,477 --> 00:21:51,694
account in a few weeks is genuinely

715
00:21:51,694 --> 00:21:52,201
painful.

716
00:21:52,381 --> 00:21:56,259
Psychologists Daniel Kahneman and Amos Tversky showed

717
00:21:56,259 --> 00:21:59,039
that losses hurt roughly twice as much

718
00:21:59,039 --> 00:22:00,868
as equal gains feel good.

719
00:22:01,048 --> 00:22:02,209
That's why people panic.

720
00:22:02,389 --> 00:22:04,605
Knowing this in advance won't remove the

721
00:22:04,605 --> 00:22:06,267
pain, but it can stop you from

722
00:22:06,267 --> 00:22:06,987
acting on it.

723
00:22:07,337 --> 00:22:08,800
And here's the good news.

724
00:22:08,980 --> 00:22:11,657
People who reach one hundred thousand often

725
00:22:11,657 --> 00:22:14,210
say the second hundred thousand came much

726
00:22:14,210 --> 00:22:14,645
faster.

727
00:22:14,825 --> 00:22:16,718
Partly because of compounding.

728
00:22:16,898 --> 00:22:18,700
But partly because by then,

729
00:22:18,700 --> 00:22:20,436
saving had become a habit,

730
00:22:20,436 --> 00:22:21,437
not a decision.

731
00:22:21,617 --> 00:22:24,021
The first hundred thousand builds the person.

732
00:22:24,201 --> 00:22:26,569
After that, the person builds the money.

733
00:22:26,919 --> 00:22:27,778
Angle three.

734
00:22:27,958 --> 00:22:28,759
The playbook.

735
00:22:28,939 --> 00:22:30,920
Here are three example paths to one

736
00:22:30,920 --> 00:22:32,335
hundred thousand dollars,

737
00:22:32,335 --> 00:22:33,920
for three different incomes.

738
00:22:34,100 --> 00:22:35,694
They're made up examples,

739
00:22:35,694 --> 00:22:37,734
but the math is simple and real.

740
00:22:38,084 --> 00:22:38,746
Path one.

741
00:22:38,926 --> 00:22:40,679
Forty thousand dollars a year.

742
00:22:40,859 --> 00:22:43,305
Alex starts with three thousand dollars of

743
00:22:43,305 --> 00:22:44,295
credit card debt.

744
00:22:44,475 --> 00:22:46,853
Six months to build a starter cushion

745
00:22:46,853 --> 00:22:48,074
and clear the card.

746
00:22:48,254 --> 00:22:50,827
Then Alex invests three hundred dollars a

747
00:22:50,827 --> 00:22:53,148
month, plus a small employer match of

748
00:22:53,148 --> 00:22:54,779
about one hundred dollars.

749
00:22:54,959 --> 00:22:57,409
Every raise, half goes to savings.

750
00:22:57,589 --> 00:22:59,695
At a seven percent illustration,

751
00:22:59,695 --> 00:23:02,592
Alex reaches one hundred thousand in roughly

752
00:23:02,592 --> 00:23:03,580
thirteen years.

753
00:23:03,760 --> 00:23:04,677
Not fast.

754
00:23:04,857 --> 00:23:07,330
But steady, and completely realistic.

755
00:23:07,680 --> 00:23:08,434
Path two.

756
00:23:08,614 --> 00:23:10,646
Seventy thousand dollars a year.

757
00:23:10,826 --> 00:23:12,876
Jordan has no debt except a car

758
00:23:12,876 --> 00:23:13,206
loan.

759
00:23:13,386 --> 00:23:15,957
Jordan builds a full emergency fund in

760
00:23:15,957 --> 00:23:18,596
a year, then invests fifteen percent of

761
00:23:18,596 --> 00:23:21,844
income, about eight hundred seventy five dollars

762
00:23:21,844 --> 00:23:23,874
a month, plus a match of about

763
00:23:23,874 --> 00:23:25,565
one hundred seventy five.

764
00:23:25,745 --> 00:23:28,211
That's roughly one thousand fifty dollars a

765
00:23:28,211 --> 00:23:28,555
month.

766
00:23:28,735 --> 00:23:30,339
At the same illustration,

767
00:23:30,339 --> 00:23:32,907
Jordan reaches one hundred thousand in a

768
00:23:32,907 --> 00:23:34,319
little over six years.

769
00:23:34,669 --> 00:23:35,435
Path three.

770
00:23:35,615 --> 00:23:38,150
A couple earning one hundred twenty thousand

771
00:23:38,150 --> 00:23:38,668
together.

772
00:23:38,848 --> 00:23:41,258
Sam and Riley keep one modest car,

773
00:23:41,258 --> 00:23:42,392
cook most meals,

774
00:23:42,392 --> 00:23:45,368
and invest twenty percent of their income,

775
00:23:45,368 --> 00:23:47,848
about two thousand dollars a month,

776
00:23:47,848 --> 00:23:49,123
including matches.

777
00:23:49,303 --> 00:23:51,261
At a seven percent illustration,

778
00:23:51,261 --> 00:23:53,831
they reach one hundred thousand dollars in

779
00:23:53,831 --> 00:23:55,422
a little under four years.

780
00:23:55,602 --> 00:23:56,824
Then they keep going,

781
00:23:56,824 --> 00:23:59,269
and the second hundred thousand comes even

782
00:23:59,269 --> 00:23:59,677
faster.

783
00:24:00,027 --> 00:24:02,257
Notice what's the same in all three

784
00:24:02,257 --> 00:24:02,639
paths.

785
00:24:02,819 --> 00:24:03,260
The order.

786
00:24:03,440 --> 00:24:05,212
Starter cushion, match,

787
00:24:05,212 --> 00:24:06,675
high interest debt,

788
00:24:06,675 --> 00:24:08,447
full cushion, automate,

789
00:24:08,447 --> 00:24:11,373
invest cheaply, control the big three,

790
00:24:11,373 --> 00:24:13,992
grow income, beat lifestyle creep,

791
00:24:13,992 --> 00:24:15,224
stay the course.

792
00:24:15,404 --> 00:24:17,065
The income changes the speed.

793
00:24:17,245 --> 00:24:19,311
The order is the same for everyone.

794
00:24:19,661 --> 00:24:21,379
And here's your one page plan.

795
00:24:21,559 --> 00:24:22,154
At the top,

796
00:24:22,154 --> 00:24:23,614
write your net worth today.

797
00:24:23,794 --> 00:24:25,524
Under it, write which step you're on.

798
00:24:25,704 --> 00:24:27,726
Under that, the one action for this

799
00:24:27,726 --> 00:24:28,072
month.

800
00:24:28,252 --> 00:24:29,184
And at the bottom,

801
00:24:29,184 --> 00:24:30,632
the date you'll check again.

802
00:24:30,812 --> 00:24:32,519
Tape it somewhere you'll see it.

803
00:24:32,699 --> 00:24:34,659
That page is worth more than any

804
00:24:34,659 --> 00:24:37,292
budgeting app, because it tells you exactly

805
00:24:37,292 --> 00:24:38,272
what to do next.

806
00:24:38,622 --> 00:24:41,122
Let's walk the whole road from zero

807
00:24:41,122 --> 00:24:42,837
to one hundred thousand,

808
00:24:42,837 --> 00:24:44,480
milestone by milestone,

809
00:24:44,480 --> 00:24:47,267
and talk about what actually changes at

810
00:24:47,267 --> 00:24:47,910
each one.

811
00:24:48,090 --> 00:24:50,219
Because knowing what's coming makes it much

812
00:24:50,219 --> 00:24:51,259
easier to keep going.

813
00:24:51,609 --> 00:24:53,106
One thousand dollars.

814
00:24:53,286 --> 00:24:55,038
This is the first milestone,

815
00:24:55,038 --> 00:24:56,665
and it's mostly emotional.

816
00:24:56,845 --> 00:24:57,761
For many people,

817
00:24:57,761 --> 00:24:59,823
it's the first time they've ever had

818
00:24:59,823 --> 00:25:01,942
money that wasn't already spoken for.

819
00:25:02,122 --> 00:25:04,582
A surprise bill no longer means a

820
00:25:04,582 --> 00:25:05,477
credit card.

821
00:25:05,657 --> 00:25:07,756
People often describe a strange,

822
00:25:07,756 --> 00:25:09,395
quiet feeling of control.

823
00:25:09,575 --> 00:25:10,539
It's small money.

824
00:25:10,719 --> 00:25:12,054
It's a big moment.

825
00:25:12,404 --> 00:25:13,704
Ten thousand dollars.

826
00:25:13,884 --> 00:25:15,626
Now you have real options.

827
00:25:15,806 --> 00:25:17,753
You could survive a few months of

828
00:25:17,753 --> 00:25:18,638
reduced income.

829
00:25:18,818 --> 00:25:20,293
You could move for a better job.

830
00:25:20,473 --> 00:25:22,127
You could say no to something that's

831
00:25:22,127 --> 00:25:22,679
bad for you.

832
00:25:22,859 --> 00:25:25,264
Ten thousand dollars is often the point

833
00:25:25,264 --> 00:25:27,176
where people stop feeling poor,

834
00:25:27,176 --> 00:25:29,520
even if their income hasn't changed at

835
00:25:29,520 --> 00:25:29,767
all.

836
00:25:29,947 --> 00:25:32,170
And at a seven percent illustration,

837
00:25:32,170 --> 00:25:34,516
ten thousand dollars can grow by about

838
00:25:34,516 --> 00:25:36,492
seven hundred dollars in a year,

839
00:25:36,492 --> 00:25:38,097
without you adding a cent.

840
00:25:38,447 --> 00:25:40,386
Twenty five thousand dollars.

841
00:25:40,566 --> 00:25:43,214
This is where many people's emergency fund

842
00:25:43,214 --> 00:25:45,485
is full and their investing is truly

843
00:25:45,485 --> 00:25:46,115
under way.

844
00:25:46,295 --> 00:25:49,024
You can absorb most of life's surprises.

845
00:25:49,204 --> 00:25:51,464
And it's often where people first notice

846
00:25:51,464 --> 00:25:53,554
their investments moving by more in a

847
00:25:53,554 --> 00:25:55,419
single day than they used to save

848
00:25:55,419 --> 00:25:55,984
in a week.

849
00:25:56,164 --> 00:25:57,389
That can be exciting,

850
00:25:57,389 --> 00:25:58,497
and a little scary.

851
00:25:58,677 --> 00:26:00,611
It's a good time to remind yourself

852
00:26:00,611 --> 00:26:02,323
that daily swings don't matter.

853
00:26:02,503 --> 00:26:03,374
Years matter.

854
00:26:03,724 --> 00:26:05,163
Fifty thousand dollars.

855
00:26:05,343 --> 00:26:05,982
Halfway.

856
00:26:06,162 --> 00:26:07,661
But here's the surprise:

857
00:26:07,661 --> 00:26:10,283
in time, you're usually more than halfway,

858
00:26:10,283 --> 00:26:13,155
because the second fifty thousand comes faster

859
00:26:13,155 --> 00:26:14,092
than the first.

860
00:26:14,272 --> 00:26:16,582
Growth starts to carry real weight.

861
00:26:16,762 --> 00:26:18,781
At a seven percent illustration,

862
00:26:18,781 --> 00:26:21,558
fifty thousand dollars can earn around three

